WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.110
By now, you know that Logan is a creative
and critical success.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.380
Because of the movie's relentless pace, it's
easy to miss out on the significance of anyone

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.410
who isn't Wolverine, Professor X, or Laura.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:17.220
Despite the brief appearances of supporting
characters, they all come with a complex comics

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.220
history.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.000
Here's a guide to the characters from Logan
with more meaning than you realized.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.540
And of course, if you haven't seen Logan yet,
avert your eyes.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.060
Donald Pierce

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.179
In Logan, Donald Pierce is the primary antagonist.

00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:37.600
While there are villains who are more powerful
and diabolical, it's the robot-handed Pierce

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.839
and his band of Reavers who relentlessly hunt
X-23 and Logan wherever they go.

00:00:42.839 --> 00:00:45.980
In Marvel's comic universe, there's much more
to Pierce.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.969
When we first meet him in Uncanny X-Men #132,
he's an exceptionally fancy cyborg member

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:55.379
of the Hellfire Club, who are nothing but
bad news for the X-Men.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:59.389
He was eventually kicked out of the Hellfire
Club, since he basically joined to kill the

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:05.991
club's mutant leaders, and in Uncanny X-Men
# 251, he reappears with The Reavers, after

00:01:05.991 --> 00:01:11.130
souping all of them up with cybernetic enhancements
and literally crucifying Wolverine on a giant

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.210
X — because Pierce is nothing if not subtle.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.010
In his comics incarnation, everything about
Pierce is cybernetic except his head, and

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.140
he can survive even if everything else has
been destroyed.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.899
All in all, this is much more impressive than
his comparitively simple robo-hand in the

00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:27.899
movie.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:29.329
The Reavers

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:34.950
Those nameless soldiers with robot parts that
follow Donald Pierce, who Wolverine and X-23

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.579
repeatedly cut into ribbons, are The Reavers.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:40.780
As far as Logan goes, those guys were pretty
weak stuff.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.710
Because, like, seriously… they were beaten
up by a little girl.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.270
And that guy literally has a gun for an arm.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.780
In the comics, however, the Reavers each have
distinctive abilities and reasons to hate

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:51.780
Wolverine.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.810
They also have totally badass late-'80s names,
like Skullbuster, Bone Breaker, Lady Deathstrike,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.810
and…

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:02.930
Pretty Boy. Pierce combines these cyborgs
with a few Hellfire mercenaries who'd been

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.080
torn apart by Wolverine and repaired with
robot parts, and the Reavers are born.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.730
Before meeting Pierce, some of them used the
teleporting mutant Gateway to commit robberies

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.019
all over the world.

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:19.489
They've also tangled with the everyone's favorite
heroic murderer in Punisher #33, but like

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:24.569
their destructible, detachable body parts,
The Reavers always come back new and improved.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:29.150
They're basically an '80s cyberpunk vision
brought to life, which probably explains why

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.150
their look was toned down for Logan.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.120
We had more than enough of that in Mad Max.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.290
Caliban

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.180
This guy continues to pop up in unexpected
places.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.530
We last saw him in X-Men: Apocalypse, where
he charges Mystique money to find mutants

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.090
and help ferry them to safety.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.970
A very different version pops up again in
Logan as someone who once used his mutant

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.950
tracking abilities to help Pierce and others
hunt mutants, but now serves as an assistant

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.080
to Charles Xavier and Logan.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.370
While he's given a moving arc in Logan, Caliban's
comic incarnation has a richer backstory.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:07.379
When we first see Caliban in Uncanny X-Men
#148, he's in the middle of a creepy misadventure

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:11.780
where he uses his super-strength to punch
Spider-Woman through a window and abduct a

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.239
teenage Kitty Pryde — mostly because he
just wants a pal.

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:20.879
Later, things get more intense in Uncanny
X-Men #179 when Caliban is tired of being

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:25.769
friendzoned and abducts Kitty and slaps her
in some kind of sewer wedding dress so he

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:26.870
can marry her.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.680
Which is not as romantic as it sounds.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.819
He eventually lets her go when he realizes
that's a really poor dating strategy.

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:35.379
Later, he ends up living with X-Factor.

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:40.909
By issue #24 of their ongoing series, Caliban,
frustrated that his tracking power doesn't

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:44.030
give him real power, asks Apocalypse for a
boost.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.010
He ends up becoming Apocalypse's Horseman
of Death, with increased strength and intelligence

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.150
thanks to genetic alterations by Apocalypse
himself.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.030
He eventually divorces Apocalypse and joins
X-Force, but only until Apocalypse brings

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.939
him back into the fold as the Horseman of
Pestilence.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:06.480
Eventually, Caliban returns back to his barely-articulate
self before getting killed in New X-Men #45.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.530
And then, because it's comics, he gets resurrected...and
killed again.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:15.430
It's a pretty complex arc of power and redemption
that Logan only kind of touches on.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:16.489
Rictor

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:21.230
Perhaps one of the most surprising faces we
see in Logan is Rictor, the de facto leader

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.420
of a group of fellow young mutants who have
also escaped experimentation.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.940
Just like his comic counterpart, he has seismic
powers and shows natural leadership abilities,

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.170
but that's all we get to see during his brief
time on screen.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:39.940
Rictor first appeared in X-Factor #17, where
he's tracked down by none other than Caliban.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:44.040
After being rescued from a group abusing his
powers, he's welcomed into X-Factor.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.570
He later joins The New Mutants by issue #77,
but leaves to join Weapon PRIME in X-Force

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.410
#11, a group whose sole purpose is hunting
down Cable — who Rictor believes killed

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.410
his father.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.450
A few issues later, he realizes the error
of his ways and joins X-Force, which is led

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.450
by Cable.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.580
The dude is pretty indecisive.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.820
As a member of just about every X-team ever,
Rictor didn't even give up fighting evil when

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.440
he lost his powers during the House of M event.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.500
He joined X-Factor Investigations under the
leadership of Jamie Madrox, the Multiple Man.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:20.240
Who, if you think about it, is probably the
worst person to have as your boss.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:21.260
X-24

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:26.700
One of the more interesting film throwbacks
in Logan is the appearance of X-24, an imperfect

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:31.120
clone of Logan, that mostly exists so we can
watch Wolverine in a mirror match against

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.120
himself.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.480
While X-24 doesn't really exist in the comics,
there are a few Marvel characters that he

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.050
may be a riff on.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:43.370
Spiritually, X-24 seems pretty similar to
Albert, a robotic duplicate of Wolverine that

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:48.690
was built by Donald Pierce in Wolverine #37
in order to kill his mutant nemesis.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.700
Albert eventually gets over his programming
and becomes an ally of Wolverine.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.960
However, the movie makes it clear that X-24
is a clone rather than a robotic duplicate,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.680
which seems to reference more recent comics
centering around X-23's early adventures as

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:03.680
the All-New Wolverine.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.900
At the start of her solo series, she encounters
a few clone versions of herself who are seeking

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:09.900
vengeance on their creators.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.790
They all have different personalities, which
seems to be a major step above the mindless

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.260
killing machine briefly portrayed in Logan.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.660
Dr. Rice &amp; Gabriela

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.740
Logan doesn't show us much of Dr. Rice, the
man behind the program that's cloning and

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:25.020
training young mutants.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.660
Pierce even credits the guy for causing the
extinction of mutants, which is a pretty big

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:30.660
deal.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:34.090
His quick death means that we don't get to
see the full depth of his relationship with

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:39.470
the young mutants, but just like the film,
the Dr. Rice of the comics is significant

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.820
to X-23… even if he's only in a few issues.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:48.020
In Marvel's comics, we first glimpse Dr. Zander
Rice as a younger scientist in X-23 #1, carrying

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.060
on his father's legacy with the Weapon X program.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:55.370
Bad science goes down when Dr. Sarah Kinney
is brought into the project, and the only

00:06:55.370 --> 00:07:00.780
way to save her work is to have an embryonic
X-23 clone popped into her womb, which she

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:01.780
brings to term.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.280
Which is a whole lot more intense than Nurse
Gabriela's role in Logan.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:10.190
That's when Dr. Rice takes over, torturing
the young Laura to bring out her mutant abilities,

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:14.500
including extracting her claws by poisoning
her with radiation, and building in a trigger

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.930
scent that drives her into a berserker rage
whenever she smells it.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:23.220
Rice uses a brainwashed Laura as a mercenary
to slaughter dozens of people, including just

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.090
about everyone in a fellow scientists' family.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:31.460
Laura ends up killing Dr. Rice and destroying
the Weapon X facility, but Rice gets his final

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:32.460
revenge.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:36.580
He manages to slip the trigger scent in Dr.
Kinney's hair, so Laura ends up murdering

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.790
the one person who ever loved her.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:43.430
As she lays dying, we see an envelope full
of information about where X-23 should go:

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.240
to visit Logan and Professor X.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.240
Sound familiar?

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.520
Thanks for watching!

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.360
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

